    "chunk_size_bytes": 1800,
    "chunk_text": "Thriposha production hindered amid maize import delay and regulatory impasse\n\nProduction of Thriposha, Sri Lanka\u2019s flagship nutritional supplement programme for combating child malnutrition and supporting pregnant and lactating mothers, has been heavily hindered due to delays in releasing a key imported maize consignment and shortages of compliant raw material, officials said.\n\nWhile production has not been fully halted, sources confirmed that output has been significantly disrupted due to the unavailability of maize meeting strict safety standards\u2014particularly aflatoxin levels below 5 parts per billion (PPB). Local supplies, officials said, often fall short of the required threshold due to gaps in post-harvest handling, storage, and testing capacity.\n\nAt the centre of the disruption is a shipment of around 2,290 metric tons of maize currently held at port, despite the importer having obtained the necessary licences and prior Cabinet approval in June 2025 under a national competitive bidding process.\n\nHowever, trade authorities have so far withheld clearance for release, insisting that fresh Cabinet approval is required. This position appears to contradict earlier guidance attributed to the Cabinet Spokesperson in January 2026, which indicated that no additional approval was necessary and that implementing agencies were already authorised to proceed.\n\nThe continued delay has raised concerns over both supply chain stability and potential quality risks. Industry sources warned that prolonged storage of maize in sealed containers under high heat and humidity could increase the risk of aflatoxin contamination, potentially rendering the stock unsuitable for human consumption. There are also concerns about pest infestation as fumigation effectiveness diminishes over time."
