    "md5": "a8eb20941ed6439237c969de4f4e3020",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1808,
    "chunk_text": "Pointers for our foreign policy in the coming new world\n\n\u201cThe old world is dying, and the new world struggles to be born: now is the time of monsters\u201d\n\nReflection on W.B.Yeats poetry, composed in the immediate aftermath of World War I, during the Russian Revolution, and amid the Irish War of Independence:\n\nSlouches towards Bethlehem to be born.\u201d (The Second Coming, 1919).\n\nThis mood of apocalypse hangs over the world today, more than two months since the start of the US/Israel war against Iran, with no end in sight. Leaders speak of the old world order as \u201cruptured\u201d ( Canadian Prime Minister at Davos), \u201cbent\u201d or \u201cgone for good\u201d(WTO Director-General Ngozi Okonjo-Iweala), so how should small developing countries navigate in the new era without the old framework of globalization, free trade, a rules based international order, reliance on UN led multilateralism?\n\nThe Canadian Prime Minister called for the middle powers to come together to offer an alternative global leadership, different coalitions for different issues based on common values and interests. Will this new global leadership benefit the developing countries? Despite the high rhetoric, we in Sri Lanka know that international human rights standards for instance, are applied selectively \u2013 Canada\u2019s criticism of Sri Lanka\u2019s conduct of its armed conflict has been coloured by their domestic politics where the Tamil migrant diaspora wields influence.\n\nBy contrast, the view from Beijing, which is receiving many high level visits these days, offers a more equitable stance on multilateralism emphasizing that developing countries should prioritize the right to economic development over civil and political liberties. Taking the lead, China has recently offered to remove tariffs on almost all African exports."
  },
  {
    "doc_type": "lk_news",
    "doc_id": "2026-05-16-islandlk-3cb62a4e",
    "num": "islandlk-3cb62a4e",
    "date_str": "2026-05-16",
    "description": "Pointers for our foreign policy in the coming new world",
