    "md5": "5081b0eca49c3dfd9e2580e74758826d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1447,
    "chunk_text": "By contrast, the view from Beijing, which is receiving many high level visits these days, offers a more equitable stance on multilateralism emphasizing that developing countries should prioritize the right to economic development over civil and political liberties. Taking the lead, China has recently offered to remove tariffs on almost all African exports.\n\nWhy theorize endlessly over non-alignment and neutrality? As far back as 1956, SWRD Bandaranaike, the first Sri Lankan Prime Minister to address the UN General Assembly, suggested standards which still stand: \u201cfriendship towards all, enmity towards none,\u201d and \u201cdynamic neutralism,\u201d advocating moral force over military power, peace, anti-colonialism and sovereignty.\n\nNAM is still relevant as we see a less known principle being tested today in the Gulf countries which had hitherto sheltered under the US security umbrella. This NAM principle prohibits the joining of regional security arrangements and the stationing of foreign troops or foreign bases on your soil. Thus in 1957 the Sri Lanka government had abrogated the 1947 United Kingdom\u2013Ceylon Defence Agreement, which had granted Britain access to military facilities including air and naval bases at Trincomalee, Katunayake (RAF Negombo), and Batticaloa. Some argue that Sri Lanka missed the opportunity to join ASEAN at its founding but at the time our leaders saw it as a regional security pact which targeted China."
  },
