    "language": "en",
    "md5": "fa3a9386c4b41496ca820c63815139f2",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1954,
    "chunk_text": "NAM is still relevant as we see a less known principle being tested today in the Gulf countries which had hitherto sheltered under the US security umbrella. This NAM principle prohibits the joining of regional security arrangements and the stationing of foreign troops or foreign bases on your soil. Thus in 1957 the Sri Lanka government had abrogated the 1947 United Kingdom\u2013Ceylon Defence Agreement, which had granted Britain access to military facilities including air and naval bases at Trincomalee, Katunayake (RAF Negombo), and Batticaloa. Some argue that Sri Lanka missed the opportunity to join ASEAN at its founding but at the time our leaders saw it as a regional security pact which targeted China.\n\nForeign policy making in any integrated far- sighted manner has been crippled by the never ending natural and man-made disasters that have hit this island, a 30-year armed conflict, a tsunami, covid, economic bankruptcy. Yet Sri Lanka has emerged from it all due to its national resilience systems and sustained cooperation with the international community. It has continued to provide examples on development, from its universal health and education systems to a unique experiment today to reduce the size of the Cabinet of Ministers. Thus Vijitha Herath is the first Foreign Minister to also head key Ministries of Foreign Employment and Tourism. This experiment where the aim is to curtail expenditure and simplify Ministerial lifestyles is welcomed by foreign donors. However the problem is that it comes at the cost of efficiency.\n\nThis style of government requires a frank admission of our diminished ability to project power abroad even as the economic crisis and domestic needs are shrinking our diplomatic footprint. A sign of this is that professional diplomats heading key posts in Paris, Berlin and Beijing are returning home at the end of their terms while new appointments, defying the diplomatic convention, are still awaited."
  },
