    "md5": "a427dfce6e8a51cd2358247cf52c4a20",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1985,
    "chunk_text": "This style of government requires a frank admission of our diminished ability to project power abroad even as the economic crisis and domestic needs are shrinking our diplomatic footprint. A sign of this is that professional diplomats heading key posts in Paris, Berlin and Beijing are returning home at the end of their terms while new appointments, defying the diplomatic convention, are still awaited.\n\nYet, going into this new era, one thing is certain, Sri Lanka must first move towards a stronger domestic economy and national self reliance. Facing the \u201clargest energy crisis in history\u201d (Fatih Birol ,the head of IEA (the International Energy Agency) , the prioritization of energy and food security, trade and finance arrangements reducing the foreign leverage that enables coercion, sustainable development to face the global climate crisis, has to come to the forefront of domestic policy making , which will shape foreign policy.\n\nIn fact, some positive results have already emerged in Sri Lanka. On energy, the crisis in the Gulf has led the government to take action to reduce dependence on costly fossil fuels and harness natural resources such as the renewables, hydro, wind and solar. Finally the government has taken the policy decision to move on storage, commissioning the 12 new battery storage BESS projects with a domestic firm. This may lead to decentralizing the power grid and sharing of power within the country, enhancing energy self sufficiency without looking to foreign firms to build huge power plants and costly plans for electricity sharing through building a grid or under sea pipelines with the Big Neighbour.\n\nAs vigils gather to remember the 40th anniversary of the Chernobyl disaster, the worst nuclear plant accident the world has known, another major policy decision should be applauded, this government\u2019s refusal of the nuclear power plant offer by Russia, heeding our local scientists who warn that the risks far outweigh the advantages."
  },
